WHAT YOU ALREADY KNOW   What you already know
Part 1 taught the translation of a foreign subsidiary and the difference between a translation adjustment in other comprehensive income and a remeasurement gain in net income. Chapter 17 taught the difference between a change in estimate and a change in principle. This chapter computes what each of them does to a ratio, and then uses every ratio in Book 1 at once.
18.1  Foreign operations and the exchange rate
A company with a subsidiary abroad must express that subsidiary's results in the currency the group reports in. Nothing about the subsidiary changes in the process, and almost every ratio can.
Start with the term the exam defines. The functional currency is the currency of the primary economic environment in which an entity operates — the one in which it prices its goods, pays its staff and holds its cash. It is a question of fact, not of choice. The presentation currency is the one the group reports in, which for Orontes is the US dollar.
Where the subsidiary's functional currency is its own local currency, the current rate method applies. Assets and liabilities are translated at the closing rate, income and expenses at the average rate for the period, and equity at historical rates. The imbalance this creates does not go through profit: it accumulates in other comprehensive income as a cumulative translation adjustment, and it is the reason the equity section of a group balance sheet carries a reserve that nobody earned.
Where the functional currency is the parent's — a subsidiary that is merely a shell operating in dollars — the temporal method applies instead. Monetary items are remeasured at the closing rate, non-monetary items stay at historical rates, and the gain or loss goes through net income. That is the distinction that matters for earnings volatility: translation is quiet and remeasurement is noisy.
WORKED EXAMPLE   Worked example: the same subsidiary, two exchange rates
Step 1. The subsidiary. A subsidiary reports sales of 10,000,000 dinars, net income of 1,000,000 dinars and total assets of 5,000,000 dinars. In a year when the dinar is stable at $0.70, sales translate at 10,000,000 × 0.70 = 7,000,000 and assets at 5,000,000 × 0.70 = 3,500,000, so asset turnover is 7,000,000 ÷ 3,500,000 = 2.00 times.
Step 2. The dinar falls. In the next year the dinar averages $0.65 and closes at $0.60, the subsidiary's local figures unchanged. Sales translate at 10,000,000 × 0.65 = 6,500,000 and assets at 5,000,000 × 0.60 = 3,000,000.
Step 3. The ratio. Asset turnover becomes 6,500,000 ÷ 3,000,000 = 2.17 times, against 2.00 the year before.
Check. The subsidiary sold the same goods to the same customers with the same assets, and its reported asset turnover rose 8 per cent. The cause is purely that the income statement is translated at an average rate and the balance sheet at a closing rate, so a currency that moved during the year puts the two halves of every mixed ratio on different bases. Ratios built entirely within the income statement — every margin in Chapter 13 — are unaffected, because the numerator and the denominator move together. Ratios built entirely within the balance sheet are unaffected for the same reason. It is the mixed ratios, which is to say every turnover and every return, that move.
Figure F518-02 states the rule.
Figure F518-02. Which ratios a rate change moves
@table
Ratio type | Example | Affected by a rate change? | Why
Income statement only | Gross margin, net margin | No | Both parts at the average rate
Balance sheet only | Current ratio, debt-to-equity | No | Both parts at the closing rate
Mixed | Asset turnover, ROA, ROE, DSO | Yes | Average rate against closing rate
Equity, under the current rate method | Book value per share | Yes | The translation adjustment sits in equity
@end
The last examinable point of this outcome is behavioural. Reported earnings that swing with a currency are earnings that management did not cause and cannot control, and the response is predictable: companies hedge, and they hedge the reported number as well as the economic exposure. Investors in turn discount volatile earnings, so a group whose profit moves with the dinar trades at a lower multiple than one whose profit does not — which is a cost of the volatility, not of the business. Book 2 prices the instruments used to manage it.
