18.2  Inflation
Inflation does to time what a currency does to distance: it makes two numbers that look comparable incomparable. A balance sheet carried at historical cost mixes dollars of many vintages, and the older the asset the more the mixing matters.
Four distortions follow, and the exam asks for their direction.
Assets are understated, so every ratio with assets in the denominator is overstated. Return on assets looks better than it is; asset turnover looks faster than it is. Orontes's own plant stands at $14,802,000 against a cost of $26,790,000, and the cost itself is in the dollars of the years in which it was spent.
Depreciation is understated, so profit is overstated. A charge computed on what an asset cost does not provide for what the replacement will cost. The company reports a profit and distributes some of it, and then finds the replacement unaffordable.
Inventory profits are not profits. Under first-in, first-out, the cost of sales carries the oldest costs and the gross margin includes the holding gain on stock that merely sat there. Last-in, first-out matches current costs against current prices and reports a lower and more honest margin — which is why the choice of inventory method matters most in exactly the periods when inflation is high.
Debt is repaid in cheaper money, so the borrower gains and the lender loses. Debt ratios computed on historical amounts understate how light an old fixed-rate borrowing has become, and a company with long-dated fixed-rate debt and short-lived assets is the one inflation treats most kindly.
WORKED EXAMPLE   Worked example: one company, two price levels
Step 1. Historical cost. A company bought its plant for 10,000,000 ten years ago, depreciates it over ten years at 1,000,000 a year, and reports a profit of 2,000,000 on net assets now standing at 5,000,000. Return on assets is 2,000,000 ÷ 5,000,000 = 40.0 per cent.
Step 2. Current cost. Replacing the plant today would cost 18,000,000, so the economically honest depreciation charge is 1,800,000 and the asset base restated to current prices is 9,000,000. Profit becomes 2,000,000 − 800,000 = 1,200,000.
Step 3. The restated return. 1,200,000 ÷ 9,000,000 = 13.33 per cent, against the 40.0 per cent reported.
Check. A return of 40 per cent and a return of 13 per cent for the same company in the same year, and the difference is entirely the price level. Nothing in US GAAP requires the second figure, and the first one is what every database will carry. This is why a long series of returns on assets for a capital-intensive company is one of the least reliable comparisons in analysis, and why an analyst comparing a company that renewed its plant last year with one that renewed it in 2005 is not comparing like with like.
Figure F518-03 collects the directions.
Figure F518-03. What inflation does to the ratios
@table
Effect | On the statements | On the ratios
Assets at old prices | Understated | Returns and turnovers overstated
Depreciation on old cost | Understated | Margins and profit overstated
First-in, first-out cost of sales | Oldest costs expensed | Gross margin overstated
Fixed-rate debt | Repaid in cheaper money | Leverage ratios overstate the real burden
@end
