18.3  Adjusting for a change in accounting treatment
The third way a ratio moves without the business moving is that the company changes how it measures something. Chapter 17 named the three kinds; this section says what to do about each before computing a ratio.
A change in accounting principle — first-in, first-out to weighted average, one revenue policy to another — is applied retrospectively. Every prior period presented is restated as though the new principle had always been used, and the cumulative effect on periods before those presented goes to opening retained earnings. For the analyst the work is already done: the comparatives in the current statements are on the new basis, and the trap is comparing them with figures taken from an older annual report, which are on the old one.
A change in accounting estimate — a useful life, an allowance, a warranty provision — is applied prospectively. Nothing is restated. So the comparatives are on the old estimate and the current year is on the new one, and the analyst must adjust if the change is large enough to matter. The note discloses the effect; the statements do not separate it.
The correction of an error is applied by restating the prior periods affected and disclosing the fact. A restatement is a signal in its own right, independent of its size: it says the control environment failed once.
WORKED EXAMPLE   Worked example: a change of inventory method, and the ratios
Step 1. The change. Suppose Orontes changed from first-in, first-out to weighted average for 2025, and the effect is to raise cost of goods sold by $300,000 and to lower closing inventory by the same amount.
Step 2. The restated figures. Gross profit becomes 14,700,000 − 300,000 = 14,400,000, so the gross margin is 14,400,000 ÷ 42,000,000 = 34.29 per cent against the 35.0 reported. Inventory becomes 2,450,000 − 300,000 = 2,150,000.
Step 3. The ratios that move, and their direction. The gross margin falls. Inventory turnover rises on both counts, because cost of sales is larger and the average inventory smaller. Days sales in inventory therefore falls. The current ratio falls, because current assets are $300,000 lower against unchanged current liabilities. Net income falls by 300,000 × 0.75 = 225,000 after tax, so every margin and every return falls with it.
Check. Six ratios moved and the company bought, made and sold exactly the same goods. Because this is a change in principle, the comparatives would also be restated, so a reader working only from the 2025 statements sees a consistent series — and a reader who takes 2024 from last year's annual report and 2025 from this one sees a margin collapse that never happened. That is the practical reason the outcome is examinable.
Figure F518-04 sets out the three treatments.
Figure F518-04. Three kinds of change, three treatments
@table
Kind of change | Treatment | What the analyst must do | Example
Accounting principle | Retrospective restatement | Use the restated comparatives, not the old report | Inventory method
Accounting estimate | Prospective | Adjust for the change where it is material | Useful life, allowance
Error | Restate and disclose | Treat the restatement itself as a finding | A misstatement corrected
@end
EXAM TRAP   The direction of an adjustment
Write down the two halves of the ratio before and after the change and compare them separately. A ratio with both parts falling can move either way, and only the proportions decide.
Where a change is retrospective, the comparatives in the current statements are already on the new basis. Where it is prospective, they are not.
A restatement for an error is never a neutral event, however small the amount.
