18.4  The limitations of ratio analysis
The limitations are examinable as a list, and the list is short enough to learn. Each entry below has appeared in Book 1 as a worked problem rather than as an assertion.
No single ratio means anything. A current ratio of 3.31 is a fact and not a verdict; Chapter 6 showed the same ratio describing a strong company and a company with unsellable stock.
A ratio needs a benchmark. Against last year, against a competitor, against a covenant, against the industry. Barada's 16 per cent gross margin against Orontes's 35 is a difference of business, not of competence, as Chapter 2 showed.
Accounting policies differ between companies. Inventory method, depreciation basis, capitalisation policy, and the whole of the GAAP-against-IFRS difference. Section 18.3 moved six of Orontes's own ratios with one policy change.
A balance sheet is one day. Seasonal businesses are measured at their quietest point, and Chapter 12 measured one company at 60, 105 and 150 days of inventory depending on the date chosen.
Ratios are historical. Every figure in Book 1 describes a year that has ended. Chapter 16's price of $9.25 is the only forward-looking number in the whole book, and it is not an accounting figure at all.
Definitions are not standardised. Chapter 14 produced four defensible returns on assets for one company in one year.
Averages hide their spread. Chapter 12's days sales outstanding of 34.3 is consistent with every customer paying on time and with one large account paying on day 120.
Window dressing is possible. A company can repay a loan on the last day of the year and redraw it on the first day of the next, which lifts the current ratio and changes nothing.
Non-financial factors are absent. The quality of management, the strength of the brand, the state of the plant, a key customer's intentions, a lawsuit. None of them is in a ratio, and any of them can matter more than all the ratios.
Figure F518-05 collects them.
Figure F518-05. Nine limitations, and where Book 1 met each
@table
Limitation | Where it appeared
No single ratio is a verdict | Chapter 6, the current ratio
A ratio needs a benchmark | Chapter 2, Barada against Orontes
Policies differ | Section 18.3, the inventory method
A balance sheet is one day | Chapter 12, the seasonal miller
Ratios are historical | Chapter 16, the only forward number is the price
Definitions are not standard | Chapter 14, four returns on assets
Averages hide spread | Chapter 12, the ageing schedule
Window dressing | Chapter 6, the year-end repayment
Non-financial factors are absent | Chapter 18, this list
@end
EXAM TRAP   Say the limitation that bites
A question asking for the limitations of ratio analysis wants the ones relevant to the data given, not a recital. Where the question hands you one balance sheet date for a seasonal business, the limitation that bites is the date. Where it hands you two companies with different inventory methods, it is the policy difference.
A limitation is not a reason to decline to answer. State the finding, then state what would change it.
