18.5  The verdict on Orontes
This is the outcome the whole book has been for: a judgment on a company's financial strength and performance, built from many ratios and expressed in a form somebody can act on. Three rules make the difference between an answer and a list.
Rank the findings by what they are worth. A two-point gross margin move on $42,000,000 of sales is $840,000 a year; the trade working capital funding Chapter 12 priced is about $272,640 a year. The first belongs in the first sentence and the second does not.
Separate the position from the performance. The balance sheet says how strong the company is now. The income statement says how well it is trading. They can disagree, and Orontes is a company where they do.
Say what would change the verdict. A verdict with no stated test is an opinion.
Figure F518-06 is the scorecard.
Figure F518-06. Orontes Foods, Inc. — the evidence, 2025
@table
Area | Key ratios | Reading
Liquidity | Current 3.31, quick 2.68, cash 1.72 | Strong, and stronger than 2024
Cash generation | Cash flow ratio 1.76, cash backing 2.54 times | Strong
Solvency | Debt-to-equity 0.55, debt-to-assets 0.36 | Conservative
Coverage | Interest cover 11.15, fixed charge 9.42 | Wide
Working capital | Cash cycle 39.8 days, DSO 34.3 | Well run, improving
Margins | Gross 35.0%, operating 9.9%, net 7.1% | Gross margin falling three years running
Returns | ROA 10.22%, ROE 16.15% | Good, and flattered by the growth
Growth | Sales 15.38% against a sustainable 13.70% | Part-funded from outside
Market | P/E 25.23, market-to-book 3.67 | Priced for growth that costs margin
@end
The verdict. Orontes is financially strong and its trading is deteriorating in one specific, measurable way.
The strength is not in doubt. It holds $7,302,100 of cash, covers its current liabilities 3.31 times and its interest 11.15 times, carries total liabilities of only $11,279,625 against equity of $20,392,975, and converts its profit into cash 2.54 times over. Its working capital is better run than a year ago on every measure in Chapters 11 and 12. On the evidence of the balance sheet alone, this company could borrow several million dollars tomorrow.
The deterioration is in the gross margin, and it is the only finding in the book worth $840,000 a year. Sales have risen 31 per cent in two years and the gross margin has fallen from 37.0 per cent to 35.0, which means the growth was bought with price. Price concessions are permanent in a way volume gains are not. Meanwhile the market has raised its multiple from 24.01 to 25.23, so the share price now embodies an expectation of continued growth that the margin trend does not support.
Two secondary findings belong in the same answer. The company grew faster than it can fund and closed the gap with a $1,500,000 share issue and an $800,000 note, which is sound once and not a policy. And 2025 contains a discretionary impairment of $2,724,000, which depresses the reported operating margin to 9.9 per cent against an underlying 16.4 — so the reported deterioration overstates the trading decline, while the margin finding understates nothing.
What would change the verdict. A gross margin of 36 per cent or better in 2026 on continued volume growth would settle the matter in the company's favour: it would show the concessions were an entry price into new customers rather than a permanent repricing. A margin of 34 per cent would confirm the opposite and would, at an unchanged multiple of 25, make the share price indefensible.
