Chapter summary
Figure F518-08 brings the chapter together.
Figure F518-08. Chapter 18 summary map: the key rule for each section.
A ratio can move with nothing happening in the business. An exchange rate does it to every mixed ratio, because the income statement translates at an average rate and the balance sheet at a closing one: an unchanged subsidiary's asset turnover went from 2.00 to 2.17 times on a falling dinar, while its margins did not move at all. Inflation does it through historical cost, understating assets and depreciation so that returns and margins are overstated — one company's return on assets was 40.0 per cent on historical figures and 13.33 per cent restated to current prices. A change of accounting treatment does it directly: a $300,000 change of inventory method moves the gross margin to 34.29 per cent and five other ratios with it, retrospectively if it is a principle and prospectively if it is an estimate. On top of those three sit the nine standing limitations, of which the ones to quote are the ones that bite on the data given. And the verdict on Orontes, built from twenty ratios: financially strong beyond argument, well run in its working capital, cash-backed, conservatively funded — and giving away two points of gross margin a year to buy growth, which is worth $840,000 a year, is the first sentence of any honest answer, and will be settled one way or the other by the 2026 margin.
Practice
Answer the multiple-choice questions first. Then try the case-style set and the written task. The answers explain why each wrong option is wrong, so read them even when you are right.
Multiple-choice questions
