B.	fall.
C.	be unchanged.
D.	become negative.
P18-04  Which ratio is NOT affected by translating a foreign subsidiary?
A.	Return on assets
B.	Total asset turnover
C.	Net profit margin
D.	Days sales outstanding
P18-05  In a period of inflation, historical-cost depreciation is:
A.	understated, so profit is overstated.
B.	overstated, so profit is understated.
C.	unaffected.
D.	restated each year under US GAAP.
P18-06  A company reports a return on assets of 40.0 per cent on historical cost. Restated to current prices, profit is 1,200,000 and assets 9,000,000. The restated return is:
A.	13.33 per cent
B.	40.0 per cent
C.	24.0 per cent
D.	7.50 per cent
P18-07  A change from first-in, first-out to weighted average is:
A.	a change in accounting principle, applied retrospectively.
B.	a change in estimate, applied prospectively.
C.	an error, requiring restatement.
D.	not permitted.
P18-08  A change raising cost of goods sold by 300,000 and lowering inventory by the same amount will lower:
A.	the gross margin and the current ratio.
B.	inventory turnover.
C.	days sales in inventory only.
D.	nothing.
P18-09  A question gives one balance sheet date for a seasonal business. The limitation that bites is:
A.	that a balance sheet is a single day.
B.	that definitions are not standardised.
C.	that non-financial factors are absent.
D.	that ratios are historical.
P18-10  Orontes's single largest finding for 2025, measured in money, is:
A.	the two points of gross margin given away, worth about 840,000 a year.
B.	the cost of funding its trade working capital, about 272,640 a year.
C.	the loss on sale of receivables of 15,000.
D.	the disposal gain of 3,000.
Case-style practice: the credit committee's question
Levant Commerce Bank's credit committee is asked to approve a $5,000,000 five-year facility for Orontes. The committee has Book 1's whole ratio set in front of it: liquidity, coverage, solvency, working capital, margins, returns, growth and the market ratios. One member observes that the company's gross margin has fallen three years running and asks whether the facility should be declined.
Required. Give the committee your recommendation. Use at least five ratios, rank the risks, and state one covenant you would attach and why. Under 300 words.
Answers and explanations are at the end of the chapter.
Answers and explanations
Section checks
SC18-1  Answer A. The functional currency is a matter of fact about where the entity operates.
B is wrong. That is the presentation currency.
C is wrong. The dollar is one possibility among many.
D is wrong. A company may be incorporated in one country and operate in another.
SC18-2  Answer B. The translation adjustment accumulates in other comprehensive income.
A is wrong. Net income carries remeasurement gains, not translation adjustments.
C is wrong. Cost of sales is unaffected.
D is wrong. It is reported through other comprehensive income, not directly in retained earnings.
SC18-3  Answer C. Both parts of the margin are translated at the average rate.
A is wrong. Turnover mixes an average-rate numerator with a closing-rate denominator.
B is wrong. The return mixes the two bases in the same way.
D is wrong. Days sales outstanding mixes a balance with a flow.
SC18-4  Answer A. Old assets at old prices give a smaller denominator.
B is wrong. Profit is overstated, not understated, because depreciation is too low.
C is wrong. The price level affects both parts of the ratio.
D is wrong. Nothing here makes a return negative.
SC18-5  Answer A. The oldest and cheapest costs flow through cost of sales.
B is wrong. That is the effect of last-in, first-out.
C is wrong. The method determines which costs are expensed.
D is wrong. The two differ precisely when prices are moving.
SC18-6  Answer A. Fixed payments are made in money worth less each year.
B is wrong. The lender receives the cheaper money.
C is wrong. The gain and loss are symmetrical, not absent.
D is wrong. A company with no debt gains nothing from this effect.
SC18-7  Answer A. A change in principle is retrospective.
B is wrong. Prospective application belongs to a change in estimate.
C is wrong. Both application and disclosure are required.
D is wrong. The comparatives themselves are restated.
SC18-8  Answer A. A larger cost of sales lowers the margin; a larger cost against a smaller average inventory raises the turnover.
B is wrong. Both directions are the wrong way round.
C is wrong. Both parts of both ratios move.
D is wrong. Inventory turnover rises.
SC18-9  Answer A. The prior periods are restated and the restatement is itself a signal.
B is wrong. Prospective treatment belongs to estimates.
C is wrong. Disclosure is required.
D is wrong. An error is not an estimate.
SC18-10  Answer C. Being computable from published statements is the strength of ratio analysis, not a limitation.
A is wrong. Ratios are indeed historical.
B is wrong. Definitions genuinely vary.
D is wrong. Non-financial factors are genuinely absent.
SC18-11  Answer A. The ratio improves at the balance sheet date and the position does not.
B is wrong. Nothing has been misstated.
C is wrong. No accounting principle has changed.
D is wrong. The transactions are real and permitted; the presentation is the problem.
SC18-12  Answer A. Ranking by money is what turns a list into a verdict.
B is wrong. Size of a ratio is not size of a finding.
C is wrong. A recital is not a verdict.
D is wrong. Limitations qualify a verdict; they are not its opening.
SC18-13  Answer A. Both are true and the margin finding is the one worth money.
B is wrong. The balance sheet evidence is strong and cannot be ignored.
C is wrong. Stopping at the strength omits the finding.
D is wrong. The outcome requires a verdict.
SC18-14  Answer A. A verdict with no stated test is an opinion.
B is wrong. Forecasting the price is not asked for.
C is wrong. The auditor is not part of the verdict.
D is wrong. The statutory rate is a detail, not a test.
Your turn
@table
Area | Answer
Liquidity | Adequate on the current ratio of 2.00 and weak behind it: a quick ratio of 0.94 means Barada cannot meet its current liabilities without selling stock.
Working capital | Poorly run and getting worse. A 44.4-day cash cycle on a 40.6-day inventory period is the profile of a distributor whose stockroom is growing faster than its sales.
Solvency | Tight. At 1.26 against a covenant limit of 1.50 there is room, but the $1,500,000 the company wants would take it to 1.74 and breach the covenant.
Margins | Thin by nature and thinning: 14.0 per cent gross against 16.0 two years ago, and 1.29 per cent net leaves no margin for error.
Growth | Above the sustainable rate by about two points a year, with no dividend to cut and no equity raised, so the gap goes on the overdraft.
Earnings quality | Low. Receivables up 38 per cent and inventory up 41 per cent on sales up 11 means the reported profit is sitting in working capital rather than in the bank.
Verdict | The position is weak behind an adequate-looking current ratio, the performance is a thin and thinning margin on growth the company cannot fund, and the test is whether the inventory can be converted to cash at book value in the next two quarters: if it can, the overdraft is a timing problem; if it cannot, a write-down at a 1.29 per cent net margin removes the whole of the year's profit.
@end
Multiple-choice questions
P18-01  Answer A. It is the currency of the primary economic environment of the entity.
B is wrong. The presentation currency is what the group reports in.
C is wrong. The parent's currency may differ from the subsidiary's functional currency.
D is wrong. It is determined by facts, not chosen.
P18-02  Answer A. Assets and liabilities translate at the closing rate.
B is wrong. The average rate applies to income and expenses.
C is wrong. Historical rates apply to equity.
D is wrong. The acquisition rate is used for specific equity components only.
P18-03  Answer A. The denominator is translated at a lower closing rate than the average applied to sales.
B is wrong. The ratio rises on a falling currency.
C is wrong. The two bases differ, so the ratio moves.
D is wrong. Neither part of the ratio becomes negative.
P18-04  Answer C. Both parts of the net margin translate at the average rate.
A is wrong. The return mixes the two bases.
B is wrong. The turnover mixes the two bases.
D is wrong. Days sales outstanding mixes a balance with a flow.
P18-05  Answer A. A charge based on old costs is too small, so profit is too large.
B is wrong. The direction is the opposite.
C is wrong. The price level is exactly what makes the charge too small.
D is wrong. US GAAP does not require price-level restatement.
P18-06  Answer A. 1,200,000 ÷ 9,000,000 = 13.33 per cent.
B is wrong. That is the historical-cost figure.
C is wrong. This has no basis in the figures.
D is wrong. This divides the restated profit by a figure that appears nowhere.
P18-07  Answer A. An inventory method is an accounting principle.
B is wrong. It is not an estimate.
C is wrong. No error has occurred.
D is wrong. Both methods are permitted.
P18-08  Answer A. A larger cost of sales lowers the margin and smaller inventory lowers current assets.
B is wrong. Inventory turnover rises.
C is wrong. Days sales in inventory falls, but it is not the only ratio affected.
D is wrong. Six ratios move.
P18-09  Answer A. One date for a seasonal business is the limitation that bites.
B is wrong. Nothing in the question turns on a definition.
C is wrong. True in general, but not the limitation the data raises.
D is wrong. True of every ratio, so it distinguishes nothing here.
P18-10  Answer A. Two points of margin on 42,000,000 of sales is about 840,000 a year.
B is wrong. That is the second-ranked finding, at about a third of the size.
C is wrong. The factoring fee is a small cost of a chosen policy.
D is wrong. A disposal gain of 3,000 is immaterial.
Case-style set: the credit committee's question
Recommendation: approve, with one covenant.
The lending case rests on five ratios and they are all comfortable. Interest coverage is 11.15 times and would remain above 6 after a $5,000,000 facility at any plausible rate. The cash flow to fixed charges ratio is 18.81 times. Debt-to-equity is 0.55 and debt-to-assets 0.36, so the company is conservatively funded and has capacity. The current ratio is 3.31 and the quick ratio 2.68, so there is no near-term liquidity risk. And cash from operations covers net income 2.54 times, so the earnings are real.
The risks, ranked. First, the gross margin: 35.0 per cent against 37.0 two years ago, worth about $840,000 a year, and the reason the company is growing. If the margin keeps falling a point a year, coverage will erode from the top of the income statement and nothing on the balance sheet will warn of it in advance. Second, growth above the sustainable rate of 13.70 per cent, which has already required a share issue and a note and will require more funding if growth continues. Third, concentration and estimate risk in the 2025 impairment of $2,724,000, which tells us the company's forecasts of its own assets have already been wrong once.
The member who asks whether to decline has identified the right risk and drawn the wrong conclusion. A falling margin on a conservatively funded, cash-generative balance sheet is a reason to attach a condition, not to refuse.
The covenant I would attach is a minimum interest coverage of 5.00 times, tested semi-annually. It is the one ratio that captures the margin risk and the funding risk in a single number, and at 11.15 times today it gives the company room to trade without giving it room to drift.
